exec(open('/home/claude/work/gita/gc.py').read())
V=[
X(1,'WHICH IS BETTER?',
 S('Arjuna pointing to a devotee before Krishna and a meditator before a formless light','A','light','FORM OR FORMLESS?'),
 'Arjuna said: Those devotees who, ever united, worship you in this way, and those who worship the imperishable, the unmanifest: which of them are the better knowers of yoga?',
 [('evam satata-yuktāḥ ye','<b>those who, ever united, thus</b>'),('bhaktāḥ tvām paryupāsate','<b>devotees, worship you</b>'),('ye ca api akṣaram avyaktam','<b>and those, the imperishable unmanifest</b>'),('teṣām ke yoga-vittamāḥ','<b>of them, who know yoga best?</b>')],
 'After the cosmic form, Arjuna asks a quiet question: should one love God with form, or meditate on the formless?',
 'This is one of the great questions of Indian spirituality: <i>saguṇa</i> (God with qualities and form) or <i>nirguṇa</i> (the formless Absolute). Śaṅkara’s Advaita honours the formless path as highest knowledge; Rāmānuja and Madhva see the personal Lord as ultimate. Kṛṣṇa’s answer in this chapter is generous to both, while preferring the easier path for embodied people.',
 'Most of us find it easier to love a face than an idea. That is not a weakness; the Gītā says it is a valid, even preferred, path.',
 'Form or formless: <b>which is better?</b>'),
X(2,'THE MOST UNITED',
 S('A devotee with mind fixed on Krishna in faith','KA','heart','YUKTATAMĀḤ'),
 'The Lord said: Those who fix their minds on me and worship me, ever united, endowed with supreme faith, I consider the most united.',
 [('mayi āveśya manaḥ ye mām','<b>who, fixing the mind on me</b>'),('nitya-yuktāḥ upāsate','<b>worship me, ever united</b>'),('śraddhayā parayā upetāḥ','<b>endowed with supreme faith</b>'),('te me yukta-tamāḥ matāḥ','<b>they I consider most united</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa answers: those who worship him with faith are the most united in yoga.',
 'This echoes 6.47, the end of the meditation chapter. Commentators differ: Rāmānuja reads it as a clear preference for devotion to the personal Lord; Śaṅkara reads it as praise of devotion for those not yet ready for the formless, without denying the formless path’s height.',
 'Faith and a mind fixed on the Divine: that is the heart of yoga.',
 'With faith, <b>the most united</b>.'),
X(3,'THOSE WHO SEEK THE FORMLESS · I',
 S('A meditator before an unmanifest, unthinkable light','Y','light','AVYAKTA'),
 '“But those who worship the imperishable, the indefinable, the unmanifest, the all-pervading, the unthinkable, the unchanging, the immovable, the eternal,”',
 [('ye tu akṣaram anirdeśyam','<b>but those who the imperishable, indefinable</b>'),('avyaktam paryupāsate','<b>the unmanifest, worship</b>'),('sarvatra-gam acintyam ca','<b>all-pervading, unthinkable</b>'),('kūṭa-stham acalam dhruvam','<b>unchanging, immovable, eternal</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa describes the other path: meditation on the formless.',
 'Eight words describe the Absolute, each a negation or a vastness. Commentators note how hard it is to hold such a goal in the mind. The sentence continues into 12.4.',
 'The formless is real, but hard to hold. Respect those who walk that path.',
 'Indefinable, <b>unthinkable</b>.'),
X(4,'THEY TOO REACH ME',
 S('Meditators with senses restrained, delighting in the good of all','Y','family','SARVA-BHŪTA-HITE'),
 '“…restraining all the senses, even-minded everywhere, delighting in the welfare of all beings: they too reach me.”',
 [('sanniyamya indriya-grāmam','<b>restraining the group of senses</b>'),('sarvatra sama-buddhayaḥ','<b>even-minded everywhere</b>'),('te prāpnuvanti mām eva','<b>they reach me alone</b>'),('sarva-bhūta-hite ratāḥ','<b>delighting in the good of all beings</b>')],
 'Those on the formless path also reach him.',
 'A generous verse: both paths arrive. Note the qualities required: self-control, equanimity, and love of all beings (see 5.25). Commentators say even the formless path is not cold; it delights in others’ good.',
 'Whatever your path, care for all beings is a sign you are on it.',
 'They too <b>reach me</b>.'),
X(5,'THE HARDER PATH',
 S('A person climbing a steep path toward a formless light','Y','mountain','KLEŚA'),
 '“Greater is the difficulty for those whose minds are set on the unmanifest; for the unmanifest goal is hard for embodied beings to reach.”',
 [('kleśaḥ adhikataraḥ teṣām','<b>greater is the difficulty for them</b>'),('avyakta-āsakta-cetasām','<b>whose minds are set on the unmanifest</b>'),('avyaktā hi gatiḥ duḥkham','<b>for the unmanifest goal is hard</b>'),('deha-vadbhiḥ avāpyate','<b>to reach for the embodied</b>')],
 'Why devotion is easier: embodied beings find the formless hard.',
 'The key word is <i>dehavat</i>, “embodied”. While we identify with a body, a goal with no form is hard to love or hold. Commentators say this is practical advice, not a ranking of truths.',
 'Choose a path that fits who you are now. You can grow from there.',
 'The formless is <b>harder for the embodied</b>.'),
X(6,'THOSE WHO OFFER ALL TO ME',
 S('Devotees offering all their actions to Krishna in meditation','KA','gift','MAT-PARĀḤ'),
 '“But those who surrender all actions to me, regarding me as the supreme goal, and worship me with undivided yoga, meditating on me,”',
 [('ye tu sarvāṇi karmāṇi','<b>but those who all actions</b>'),('mayi sannyasya mat-parāḥ','<b>surrendering to me, intent on me</b>'),('ananyena eva yogena','<b>with undivided yoga</b>'),('mām dhyāyantaḥ upāsate','<b>meditating on me, worship</b>')],
 'The devotee’s path: offering all action, with God as goal.',
 'This joins karma yoga (offering action), dhyāna (meditation) and bhakti (devotion) into one practice. The sentence continues into 12.7.',
 'Offer your work, keep God as your aim, and meditate with love.',
 'All actions <b>offered to me</b>.'),
X(7,'I LIFT THEM OUT',
 S('Krishna lifting a devotee out of a stormy sea','K','ocean','SAMUDDHARTĀ'),
 '“…for them, whose minds are set on me, I become before long the one who lifts them out of the ocean of death and rebirth, Pārtha.”',
 [('teṣām aham samuddhartā','<b>for them I am the deliverer</b>'),('mṛtyu-saṃsāra-sāgarāt','<b>from the ocean of death and rebirth</b>'),('bhavāmi na cirāt pārtha','<b>I become before long</b>'),('mayi āveśita-cetasām','<b>of those whose minds are set on me</b>')],
 'A promise: God himself rescues the devotee.',
 'The image of <i>saṃsāra</i> as an ocean is old and powerful. Commentators of the bhakti traditions stress the active role of grace: God does the lifting. Compare 9.22.',
 'You don’t have to swim the whole ocean alone. Trust that help comes.',
 'I lift them <b>out of the sea</b>.'),
X(8,'FIX YOUR MIND ON ME',
 S('Arjuna with mind and intellect fixed on Krishna','KA','heart','MAYI EVA'),
 '“Fix your mind on me alone; let your intellect dwell in me. You will then live in me hereafter; of this there is no doubt.”',
 [('mayi eva manaḥ ādhatsva','<b>fix your mind on me alone</b>'),('mayi buddhim niveśaya','<b>let your intellect enter me</b>'),('nivasiṣyasi mayi eva','<b>you will live in me</b>'),('ataḥ ūrdhvam na saṃśayaḥ','<b>hereafter, no doubt</b>')],
 'The first and highest method: mind and intellect fixed on God.',
 'Verses 12.8–11 give a ladder of methods, each for those who cannot do the one before. Commentators call this one of the most practical passages in the Gītā: there is a path for everyone.',
 'Begin with the highest. If it’s too hard, take the next step down. There is always a step.',
 'Mind and intellect <b>in me</b>.'),
X(9,'IF NOT, PRACTISE',
 S('A person returning to practice again and again','Y','mind','ABHYĀSA'),
 '“If you cannot fix your mind steadily on me, then seek to reach me by the yoga of practice, Dhanañjaya.”',
 [('atha cittam samādhātum','<b>if the mind to fix</b>'),('na śaknoṣi mayi sthiram','<b>you cannot, steadily on me</b>'),('abhyāsa-yogena tataḥ','<b>then by the yoga of practice</b>'),('mām icchā āptum dhanañjaya','<b>seek to reach me</b>')],
 'Step two: if the mind won’t stay, practise.',
 '<i>Abhyāsa</i>, repeated practice, appeared in 6.35. Commentators: bring the mind back again and again, through japa, meditation, worship.',
 'Repetition builds habit. A little daily practice is enough to begin.',
 'If not, <b>practise</b>.'),
X(10,'IF NOT, WORK FOR ME',
 S('A person doing service as work for Krishna','P','gift','MAT-KARMA'),
 '“If you are unable even to practise, be intent on doing work for me. Even by doing actions for my sake, you will reach perfection.”',
 [('abhyāse api asamarthaḥ asi','<b>if you are unable even to practise</b>'),('mat-karma-paramaḥ bhava','<b>be intent on work for me</b>'),('mat-artham api karmāṇi','<b>even actions for my sake</b>'),('kurvan siddhim avāpsyasi','<b>doing, you will reach perfection</b>')],
 'Step three: if practice is hard, serve.',
 'Service, <i>sevā</i>, is accessible to anyone: cleaning a temple, helping people, doing one’s job as an offering. Commentators say this too leads to perfection.',
 'If sitting still is hard, serve. Service is also a path.',
 'If not, <b>serve</b>.'),
X(11,'IF NOT, LET GO OF RESULTS',
 S('A person working and letting go of a bag of rewards','P','seed','PHALA-TYĀGA'),
 '“If you cannot do even this, then, taking refuge in my yoga and controlling yourself, give up the fruit of all action.”',
 [('atha etat api aśaktaḥ asi','<b>if you are unable even this</b>'),('kartum mat-yogam āśritaḥ','<b>to do, relying on my yoga</b>'),('sarva-karma-phala-tyāgam','<b>giving up the fruit of all action</b>'),('tataḥ kuru yata-ātmavān','<b>then do, self-controlled</b>')],
 'Step four, open to all: just let go of the results.',
 'This is karma yoga at its simplest (2.47). Commentators note the ladder ends where the Gītā began: anyone, anywhere, can let go of fruits.',
 'Even if nothing else is possible, you can do your work and let go of the outcome.',
 'If not, <b>let go of the fruit</b>.'),
X(12,'GIVING UP FRUIT BRINGS PEACE',
 S('A ladder: practice, knowledge, meditation, letting go, peace at the top','','mountain','TYĀGĀT ŚĀNTIḤ'),
 '“Knowledge is better than practice; meditation is better than knowledge; giving up the fruit of action is better than meditation; for peace follows giving up.”',
 [('śreyaḥ hi jñānam abhyāsāt','<b>knowledge is better than practice</b>'),('jñānāt dhyānam viśiṣyate','<b>meditation excels knowledge</b>'),('dhyānāt karma-phala-tyāgaḥ','<b>giving up fruit, than meditation</b>'),('tyāgāt śāntiḥ anantaram','<b>from giving up, peace immediately</b>')],
 'A surprising ranking: letting go of fruit leads directly to peace.',
 'Commentators wrestle with this ordering, since 12.11 seemed to make letting go the lowest step. One reading: practice without understanding is less than knowledge; knowledge without absorption is less than meditation; but letting go brings immediate peace, the fruit of all. Śaṅkara says the verse praises renunciation of fruit to encourage those on the simple path.',
 'Letting go is not the easy option. It is the doorway to peace.',
 'Letting go <b>brings peace</b>.'),
X(13,'HATING NONE',
 S('A devotee smiling at all beings, friend to everyone','Y','family','ADVEṢṬĀ'),
 '“One who hates no being, who is friendly and compassionate, free from ‘mine’ and from ego, the same in pain and pleasure, forgiving,”',
 [('adveṣṭā sarva-bhūtānām','<b>hating no being</b>'),('maitraḥ karuṇaḥ eva ca','<b>friendly and compassionate</b>'),('nirmamaḥ nirahaṅkāraḥ','<b>free of “mine” and ego</b>'),('sama-duḥkha-sukhaḥ kṣamī','<b>same in pain and joy, forgiving</b>')],
 'Verses 12.13–20 describe the devotee dear to God.',
 'These eight verses are among the most recited in the Gītā. Note the first mark: <i>adveṣṭā</i>, hating no one. Then <i>maitrī</i> and <i>karuṇā</i>, friendship and compassion, words also central in Buddhism. Commentators say these qualities are both the fruit and the practice of devotion.',
 'Hate no one; be friendly and kind. That is where devotion shows.',
 'Hating none, <b>friendly to all</b>.'),
X(14,'CONTENT AND STEADY',
 S('A content devotee, mind and intellect offered to Krishna','Y','heart','SANTUṢṬAḤ'),
 '“…ever content, a yogi, self-controlled, of firm resolve, with mind and intellect offered to me: that devotee of mine is dear to me.”',
 [('santuṣṭaḥ satatam yogī','<b>ever content, a yogi</b>'),('yata-ātmā dṛḍha-niścayaḥ','<b>self-controlled, firm in resolve</b>'),('mayi arpita-manaḥ-buddhiḥ','<b>mind and intellect offered to me</b>'),('yaḥ mat-bhaktaḥ saḥ me priyaḥ','<b>that devotee of mine is dear to me</b>')],
 'The refrain begins: “that devotee is dear to me”.',
 'Each verse in this section ends with “dear to me”. Commentators find it moving: God speaks of what he loves in a person.',
 'Contentment, steadiness, offering: these make us dear to the Divine.',
 '<b>Dear to me</b>.'),
X(15,'NOT DISTURBING, NOT DISTURBED',
 S('A calm person in a busy crowd, not troubling anyone and not troubled','Y','light','UNDISTURBED'),
 '“One by whom the world is not disturbed, and who is not disturbed by the world; who is free from joy, envy, fear and anxiety: he is dear to me.”',
 [('yasmāt na udvijate lokaḥ','<b>by whom the world is not disturbed</b>'),('lokāt na udvijate ca yaḥ','<b>who is not disturbed by the world</b>'),('harṣa-amarṣa-bhaya-udvegaiḥ','<b>from elation, envy, fear, anxiety</b>'),('muktaḥ yaḥ saḥ ca me priyaḥ','<b>who is free, is dear to me</b>')],
 'A two-way peace: neither troubling others nor troubled by them.',
 'A beautiful ideal for any community. Commentators note <i>amarṣa</i>, impatience or envy at others’ success, listed with fear and anxiety.',
 'Be a calm presence. Don’t disturb others, and don’t let them disturb your peace.',
 'Not disturbing, <b>not disturbed</b>.'),
X(16,'PURE AND UNCONCERNED',
 S('A pure, skilful, unconcerned devotee who has let go of selfish undertakings','Y','light','ŚUCIḤ DAKṢAḤ'),
 '“One who expects nothing, is pure, skilful, unconcerned, untroubled, and has given up all selfish undertakings: that devotee of mine is dear to me.”',
 [('anapekṣaḥ śuciḥ dakṣaḥ','<b>expecting nothing, pure, skilful</b>'),('udāsīnaḥ gata-vyathaḥ','<b>unconcerned, untroubled</b>'),('sarva-ārambha-parityāgī','<b>giving up all (selfish) undertakings</b>'),('yaḥ mat-bhaktaḥ saḥ me priyaḥ','<b>that devotee is dear to me</b>')],
 'Pure, skilful, free of expectation.',
 'Note <i>dakṣa</i>, skilful or capable: the devotee is not a dreamer but competent. Commentators read “giving up undertakings” as giving up selfish ventures, not all work.',
 'Be competent and free of expectation. Both together are rare and beautiful.',
 'Pure, skilful, <b>expecting nothing</b>.'),
X(17,'BEYOND GOOD AND BAD FORTUNE',
 S('A devotee calm before both good and bad fortune','Y','scale','ŚUBHA-AŚUBHA'),
 '“One who neither rejoices nor hates, neither grieves nor craves, who has given up both good and bad fortune, full of devotion: he is dear to me.”',
 [('yaḥ na hṛṣyati na dveṣṭi','<b>who neither rejoices nor hates</b>'),('na śocati na kāṅkṣati','<b>neither grieves nor craves</b>'),('śubha-aśubha-parityāgī','<b>giving up good and bad</b>'),('bhaktimān yaḥ saḥ me priyaḥ','<b>full of devotion, dear to me</b>')],
 'Four reactions released: rejoicing, hating, grieving, craving.',
 'Commentators read “giving up good and bad” as renouncing the fruits of both good and bad deeds: freedom from all karmic accounting.',
 'Let go of the scoreboard of good and bad fortune. Love remains.',
 'Beyond <b>good and bad fortune</b>.'),
X(18,'FRIEND AND FOE ALIKE',
 S('A devotee standing calmly in heat and cold, honour and insult','Y','scale','SAMAḤ'),
 '“The same to friend and foe, in honour and dishonour, in cold and heat, in pleasure and pain, free from attachment,”',
 [('samaḥ śatrau ca mitre ca','<b>the same to foe and friend</b>'),('tathā māna-apamānayoḥ','<b>and in honour and dishonour</b>'),('śīta-uṣṇa-sukha-duḥkheṣu','<b>in cold, heat, pleasure, pain</b>'),('samaḥ saṅga-vivarjitaḥ','<b>the same, free from attachment</b>')],
 'Equanimity in every pair of opposites.',
 'This echoes 6.7–9. The sentence continues into 12.19. Commentators: equanimity is not indifference but a heart that does not swing.',
 'Treat friend and foe with the same fairness. It is a strength.',
 'The same to <b>friend and foe</b>.'),
X(19,'SILENT AND CONTENT',
 S('A quiet devotee with no fixed home, content with anything','Y','light','MAUNĪ'),
 '“…the same in blame and praise, silent, content with whatever comes, without a fixed home, steady in mind, full of devotion: that person is dear to me.”',
 [('tulya-nindā-stutiḥ maunī','<b>same in blame and praise, silent</b>'),('santuṣṭaḥ yena kenacit','<b>content with whatever</b>'),('aniketaḥ sthira-matiḥ','<b>homeless, steady-minded</b>'),('bhaktimān me priyaḥ naraḥ','<b>devoted, that person is dear to me</b>')],
 'Silent, content, unattached to a home.',
 '<i>Aniketa</i>, without a fixed home, is read by some literally (wandering monks) and by others as unattached to one’s home. <i>Maunī</i>, silent: speaking little and wisely.',
 'Speak less, want less. Contentment grows in quiet.',
 'Silent, content, <b>steady</b>.'),
X(20,'EXCEEDINGLY DEAR',
 S('Devotees drinking nectar of this teaching, Krishna embracing them','KA','heart','ATĪVA ME PRIYĀḤ'),
 '“But those who, with faith, regarding me as supreme, follow this immortal dharma as I have taught it: those devotees are exceedingly dear to me.”',
 [('ye tu dharma-amṛtam idam','<b>but those who this nectar of dharma</b>'),('yathā uktam paryupāsate','<b>as taught, follow</b>'),('śraddadhānāḥ mat-paramāḥ','<b>with faith, me as supreme</b>'),('bhaktāḥ te atīva me priyāḥ','<b>those devotees are exceedingly dear</b>')],
 'Those who even try to follow this are “exceedingly dear”.',
 'A tender ending: those who are still striving, with faith, are even dearer than “dear”. Commentators note the encouragement: perfection is not required, only sincere following.',
 'You don’t have to be perfect to be loved. Sincere effort with faith is enough.',
 '<b>Exceedingly dear</b>.'),
]
cover=('<svg width="380" height="268" viewBox="0 0 380 268" role="img" aria-label="Krishna embracing a devotee who offers a lotus, with people of all kinds around them in friendship">'
 '<rect width="380" height="268" fill="#0b2a4a" rx="8"/>'+GLOW(190,130,110,'#ffd60a',.25)+'<path d="M0 230 h380 V268 H0z" fill="#2a7a4a" stroke="#111"/>'+KR(220,180,1.5)+AR(150,200,1.2,'folded',False)+MOUNT('lotus',180,190,1.2)+''.join(person(40+k*30,236,c,'#c98b5a',.8) for k,c in enumerate(['#ff8c1a','#2a9d6f','#c0392b']))+''.join(person(290+k*30,236,c,'#c98b5a',.8) for k,c in enumerate(['#1d6fb8','#ffd60a','#9b5de5']))+'</svg>')
P=dict(ch=12,nv=20,V=V,per=[20],out='/home/claude/work/gita/Gita_Chapter12.html',
 title='Devotion · Bhagavad Gītā Chapter 12 · srini.presents',
 desc='Bhagavad Gītā chapter 12, Bhakti Yoga, every śloka explained: form or formless, the ladder of practice, service and letting go, and the marks of the devotee dear to the Lord: hating none, friendly, compassionate, the same to friend and foe. All 20 ślokas in IAST, each on two pages.',
 burst='CHAPTER 12 OF 18',h1='DEVOTION',series='BHAGAVAD GĪTĀ · CHAPTER 12 · BHAKTI YOGA',tag='The devotee dear to me · every śloka explained',cover=cover,
 where=[f'In {chl(11)} Arjuna saw the cosmic form and was told: only by undivided devotion can I be known and seen.',
  'Chapter 12, the shortest, is about that devotion. Arjuna asks: is it better to love God with form or to meditate on the formless? Kṛṣṇa answers, then gives a ladder of practices for every kind of person, and ends with a famous portrait of the devotee who is dear to him.'],
 wtalk=("The shortest chapter?","Twenty verses, and some of the most loved. Many people learn 12.13–20 by heart."),
 fullnote=FULLNOTE,
 secs=[('FORM OR FORMLESS',1),('A LADDER OF PRACTICE',8),('THE DEVOTEE DEAR TO ME',13)],
 end=['<b>Form or formless (12.1–5).</b> Both reach him; devotion to the personal Lord is easier for embodied beings.',
  '<b>Rescue (12.6–7).</b> Those who offer all to him he lifts out of the ocean of rebirth.',
  '<b>The ladder (12.8–12).</b> Fix the mind on me; if not, practise; if not, serve; if not, let go of the fruit; peace follows.',
  '<b>Dear to me (12.13–19).</b> Hating none, friendly, compassionate, content, not disturbing or disturbed, the same to friend and foe.',
  '<b>Exceedingly dear (12.20).</b> Those who follow this nectar of dharma with faith.'],
 endsub='Chapter 12 in five lines',
 next=("What's next?",f'{chl(13)}: The field and the knower of the field. The last six chapters turn to knowledge: what is the body (the field), who is the knower, and how to tell them apart.'),
 endkey='<b>hate none, love all, and you are dear to the Lord</b>.',
 foot=FOOT(12))
build2(P)
